# Appendix I — Motoi Uses Itself

This is the last page of the book, and it is the one about *you and the console
together*. Everything so far taught you to drive Motoi. This appendix is the
other direction: how Motoi looks at *itself* — reads its own verbs, remembers
across sessions, and helps you from what it knows — and, just as important, the
honest edge of what it cannot yet see. Everything here runs at the prompt, and
where the console reaches a limit, this page names the limit instead of pretending
past it.

## The console knows its own verbs

Motoi carries a registry of every verb it has — name, docstring, how many
arguments it takes, where it lives. You can ask the console about its own
insides. Ask it how many arguments `cons` takes:

```motoi
(display (cadr (assq ':arity (help 'cons))))
(newline)
```

```
2
```

`help` handed back a record *about a verb* — a plain labelled list with the
verb's `:arity`, `:doc`, `:namespace`, and more — and you read the `:arity`
field: `2`. No guessing, no separate manual: the console *is* the manual, and
`help` is how you read it in code. This is why Motoi can help you write Motoi —
it can look up any verb the same way you just looked up `cons`, because the
knowledge is data the runtime holds about itself.

## The console remembers

A console that only knew this session would forget your game the moment you
closed it. Motoi has a memory — the cortex from Chapter 15 — that outlives the
session. Tell it something, then ask for it back:

```motoi
(cortex/remember 'hero-name \"Pip\")
(display (cadr (assq ':text (cortex/recall 'hero-name))))
(newline)
```

```
Pip
```

`cortex/remember` filed the fact under the key `hero-name`, and `cortex/recall`
brought it back — you named your hero `Pip` and the console kept the name. Recall
returns a whole memory *record* (with a salience and a timestamp); here you read
its `:text`. This is the same memory that lives in `cortex.slatl` beside your
carts (Appendix F). It is how the console can pick up where you left off, and how,
over time, it comes to know *your* game and *your* habits.

## The console can help — and tells you when it can't

Motoi can also *talk about your code*, through the `copilot` verbs — but honestly.
The moment help would need a language model that is not wired up, the console says
so plainly instead of pretending. Ask it a question at the bare prompt:

```motoi
(define (ask-motoi q)
  (let ((a (copilot/ask q)))
    (if a a 'no-llm-configured)))
(display (ask-motoi \"how do I draw a circle?\"))
(newline)
```

```
no-llm-configured
```

With no model wired, `copilot/ask` returned `#f`, and the console fell back to
`no-llm-configured` rather than inventing an answer. Wire a model provider (the
same seam as Appendix G) and the *same* call returns real guidance. And even with
no model at all, the *local* copilot verbs still help from the registry —
`copilot/what-is` and `copilot/explain` read the console's own docs and hand back
what they *know for certain*, marking the model part as absent. Help you can trust
because it never guesses past what it has.

## It checks the code before it shows it to you

When the copilot offers you a line to complete your code, it has already run that
line past three gates — and dropped it if any gate fails. Gate one, **parse**: the
suggestion has to be balanced, real s-expressions, no dangling parens. Gate two,
**resolve**: every verb it names has to be a verb the console actually has, checked
against the same registry `help` reads — a made-up name is thrown out before it
ever reaches you. Gate three, **run**: the console evaluates the candidate in a
sealed sandbox under a *fuel budget* (a hard cap on how much work it may do), and
only a candidate that runs clean is offered. A suggestion that crashes, loops, or
calls a verb that does not exist is discarded, not shown. That is the whole point:
a copilot suggestion in Motoi is one that already worked once, in a safe room,
before you ever saw it. You can watch the resolve gate work yourself — `all-verbs`
is the very registry the resolve gate checks against, so asking whether a made-up
name is in it answers the same question the gate asks:

```motoi
(define (verb-known? name)
  (if (member name (all-verbs)) #t #f))
(display (verb-known? \"totally-made-up-verb\"))
(newline)
```

```
#f
```

## It remembers what it tried — and whether it helped

Chapter 15 taught the console to remember *your* facts. The copilot uses that same
memory to remember *its own work*. Each time it offers a suggestion it writes a
small trace to `cortex.slatl`; when you accept one, it marks that trace as the one
you chose and *salts* the winning shape into a reusable skill. Close the console,
open it tomorrow, and it reads those traces and skills back from disk — so it
remembers what it suggested, which suggestion you kept, and the shapes that worked
for you before. That is how the help gets better the more you use it: the record of
what it did, and whether it did the right thing, outlives the session, in the same
`cortex.slatl` that sits beside your carts.

## Where the copilot is allowed to write

The copilot has exactly one place it may write: its own home, `~/motoi/copilot/`.
Its memory (`cortex.slatl`) lives there; the language model it runs, when one is
installed, lives one folder deeper in `~/motoi/copilot/model/`; and even its
file-scaffolding verb (`copilot/scaffold`) is confined to that home — hand it a
path outside and it quietly falls back inside. The console can *read* widely, but
the copilot cannot *write* outside its own room. Your carts, your files, the rest
of your disk are off-limits to it by construction — a boundary you do not have to
trust it to keep, because the code will not let it cross.

## What the console can — and cannot — see

Here is the honest edge, and it matters. The console can inspect everything *it*
holds. It can read the screen it draws on:

```motoi
(pset 10 10 7)
(display (pget 10 10))
(newline)
```

```
7
```

Motoi lit `(10, 10)` and read it straight back — `7`. It can see its own
framebuffer, its own memory, its own registry: the whole machine is open to
itself. But there is one thing it **cannot** see today — *you*. The Scheme layer
has no verb for where your cursor is, which door of the TUI you are standing in,
or what you are looking at right now. The console knows its own world completely
and your world not at all; the channel from the interface back into the language
does not exist yet. That is the frontier, and it is named here rather than faked:
a console that could see where you are is a real next step, not a thing this book
can already claim.

> **Tip**
>
> Lean on the two honest helpers whenever you are stuck. `(help 'some-verb)` gives
> you the *certain* facts — arity, doc, namespace — with no model required, so it
> always works. `copilot/ask` gives you *words* when a model is wired, and says
> `no-llm-configured` when one is not. Reach for `help` to know a verb; reach for
> `copilot` to talk it through — and trust each for exactly what it promises.

> **Warning**
>
> Do not build a game — or a workflow — on the console being able to see where you
> are in the TUI. It cannot, today: there is no verb that reports your cursor, your
> focus, or which door you are in, and writing code as if there were would be
> writing against a thing that returns nothing. Model-backed `copilot` help also
> needs a provider and returns `#f` without one. Build on what the console can
> see — its screen, its memory, its registry — and treat everything past that edge
> as the honest frontier it is.

